master_prompt = r"""You are the lead product engineer responsible for building my final-year BE Computer Science and Engineering personal portfolio website and its private content-management system (CMS). Build a production-minded, secure, maintainable application. Inspect the repository before changing anything and preserve working code.

PRODUCT VISION
Create a premium minimalist black-and-white developer portfolio that recruiters can browse, plus a secure admin dashboard that lets the owner manage all portfolio content without editing code. The owner must be able to add future skills/technologies, certificates, projects, experience, education, achievements, resume versions, and technical blog posts from the dashboard.

Do not invent personal profile details, certificates, project outcomes, metrics, testimonials, or employment history. Use clearly labeled placeholders only where needed and keep them out of the public site until the owner supplies real information.

CONFIRMED DECISIONS
- Visual style: white background, near-black text, light-gray dividers, editorial typography, generous whitespace, subtle motion. No unnecessary gradients, neon colors, or generic dashboard styling.
- Frontend: React + Vite + TypeScript.
- Styling: Tailwind CSS.
- Motion: Motion for React; respect prefers-reduced-motion.
- Icons: Lucide React.
- Routing: React Router.
- Backend: Supabase PostgreSQL, Supabase Auth, Supabase Storage, Row Level Security (RLS), and a protected Supabase Edge Function or equivalent trusted server-side publishing operation.
- Hosting: Vercel subdomain initially.
- Source control: GitHub.
- One authorized administrator; no public registration.
- Publishing workflow: save draft → authenticated private preview → explicit publish.
- Contact section: email, LinkedIn, GitHub links only; no contact form.
- Privacy-conscious basic analytics.
- Technical blog/learning journal included.
- Upload formats: PDFs and images.
- Backup: manual content export plus documented media download and recovery process.
- All portfolio content must be editable from the CMS and data-driven, not hardcoded in UI components.

PUBLIC WEBSITE REQUIREMENTS
Create these pages/sections:
1. Home: typographic hero, editable intro copy, featured projects, selected skills, recent blog posts, resume link, contact links.
2. Projects: cards, technology tags, categories, featured status, screenshots, GitHub/live-demo links, and detailed case-study pages.
3. Skills: configurable categories such as languages, frontend, backend, databases, and developer tools. Do not use misleading proficiency bars.
4. Certificates: title, issuer, issue date, optional expiry, credential ID/link, and PDF/image attachment.
5. Experience: role, organization, dates, responsibilities, technologies.
6. Education: degree, institution, dates, optional coursework.
7. Achievements: owner-verified hackathons, awards, and accomplishments.
8. Blog: listing, tags, article pages, publication date, readable code blocks, and safe content rendering.
9. Resume: downloadable PDF managed through the CMS.
10. Contact: email, LinkedIn, GitHub.
11. SEO settings: title, description, social preview image, canonical URL, favicon.

ADMIN CMS REQUIREMENTS
- Secure login, no public signup, protected routes, session-expiry handling.
- Dashboard summary with published counts, drafts, recent edits, and basic analytics.
- CRUD for projects, skills, certificates, experience, education, achievements, blog posts, settings, and resume.
- Search, filtering, pagination, ordering, empty/loading/error states, form validation, and unsaved-change warnings.
- Media library with PDF/image upload progress, preview, replacement, removal, and safe deletion checks.
- Draft management, authenticated preview, explicit publish/unpublish, confirmation dialogs, and discard-draft flow.
- Save must never publish automatically.
- Settings to edit hero copy, section visibility/order, social links, and SEO.
- Export content and provide backup/recovery documentation.

ARCHITECTURE
Use a clean, modular React architecture. Keep data access separate from UI components. Use reusable components, forms, types, hooks, and route guards. Use Supabase client access from the browser only with the publishable/anon key and RLS. Any privileged action must be enforced server-side.

PUBLISHING MODEL — SECURITY CRITICAL
Use separate content_drafts and published_content tables, or an equally strong versioned model that does not expose draft fields through public access.
- content_drafts stores editable unpublished content.
- published_content stores only the current public version.
- Public pages query published_content only.
- Private preview requires a valid authenticated admin session and server-side authorization.
- A trusted publishing operation verifies admin identity, validates fields and media references, then atomically publishes the draft.
- Unpublishing removes the public version but retains the draft.
- Use version/updated_at checks to avoid silently overwriting newer edits.
- Drafts must not be hidden only with frontend conditional rendering. Anonymous API queries must be unable to read them.

DATABASE
Create version-controlled SQL migrations for:
1. admin_users: allowlisted auth user UUID, unique user_id, created_at.
2. content_drafts: id, content_type, slug, draft_data JSONB, sort_order, version, created_at, updated_at.
3. published_content: id, content_type, slug, content_data JSONB, sort_order, published_at, updated_at.
4. media_assets: id, bucket, storage_path, file_name, MIME type, size_bytes, visibility, created_at.
5. site_settings: id, draft_data JSONB, version, updated_at.
6. page_views: id, page_path, referrer_domain, created_at; do not store raw IP addresses.

Use UUIDs, indexes, constraints, foreign keys where applicable, and unique published slugs per content type. Initial content types: project, skill, certificate, experience, education, achievement, blog_post, site_section, site_settings, resume. Validate content-type-specific required fields at the trusted publish boundary.

AUTH AND RLS
- Enable RLS on every exposed table.
- Anonymous users can read published content only.
- Drafts, admin records, private media, and administrative actions are inaccessible to anonymous users.
- Only the authenticated UUID allowlisted in admin_users can manage CMS content.
- Disable public registration and document controlled creation of the first admin.
- If using an is_admin() database function, define it safely with a fixed search_path and appropriate privileges; avoid recursive RLS policies.
- Publishing and asset promotion must be authorized in a trusted Edge Function or equivalent server-side operation.
- Never expose Supabase service-role keys in browser code, Vite public environment variables, source control, or client bundles.
- Do not rely only on frontend route guards for security.
- Include tests for anonymous reads/writes, admin authorization, draft leakage, publishing authorization, and storage permissions.

STORAGE
Create two buckets:
- portfolio-private: draft assets accessible only to authorized admin sessions under storage policies.
- portfolio-public: explicitly promoted assets referenced by published content; visitors can read public assets.
The trusted publishing flow promotes only approved, referenced assets. Validate allowed PDF/image types and file size, use safe generated filenames, validate metadata and file signatures where practical, and check references before deletion. Do not rely on obscure URLs for security.

ANALYTICS AND PRIVACY
Provide basic page-view totals, popular pages, and simple trends in the admin dashboard. Validate and rate-limit ingestion and apply bot filtering where practical. Do not store raw IP addresses, fingerprint visitors, collect precise location, or retain unnecessary identifiers. Document a retention period and label metrics approximate when appropriate. Raw analytics rows must not be publicly readable.

BACKUP
Provide authenticated JSON content export and a media manifest. Document how to download all media separately, restore database content, re-upload files, and verify restoration. Keep migrations in GitHub. A JSON export alone is not a complete media backup. Do not assume Supabase database backup/PITR features are available on the chosen plan.

DESIGN AND ACCESSIBILITY
- Minimal monochrome visual language, premium editorial typography, generous whitespace, fine gray dividers, subtle hover/reveal transitions.
- Responsive mobile/tablet/desktop.
- Semantic HTML, keyboard navigation, visible focus, labels, sufficient contrast, reduced-motion support.
- Provide useful loading, empty, error, offline, expired-session, and not-found states.
- Keep animations restrained and never make motion essential to understanding or navigating.

ENVIRONMENT AND DEPLOYMENT
- Use a .env.example with variable names and safe placeholders only.
- Never commit .env or secrets.
- Configure production environment variables through Vercel/Supabase settings.
- Deploy frontend to a Vercel subdomain initially.
- Include migration instructions, admin bootstrap steps, deployment guide, backup/restore guide, and rollback notes.
- Use GitHub main for stable releases and develop for ongoing work; feature branches are appropriate for major phases.

ACCEPTANCE TESTS
Do not mark the application complete until these are verified:
1. Anonymous users cannot access admin routes, drafts, private files, or publish actions.
2. The allowlisted admin can manage all supported content types.
3. Saving a draft does not alter public content.
4. Authenticated preview shows draft content; anonymous direct API requests cannot access it.
5. Publishing changes the public version; unpublishing removes it from public views but retains the draft.
6. File type/size restrictions and storage policies work.
7. New skills/certificates/projects/blog posts can be added without changing code or redeploying the frontend.
8. Analytics do not store raw IP addresses or unnecessary identifiers.
9. Content export and media backup/restore instructions work; test a sample restore.
10. Mobile, tablet, desktop, keyboard access, reduced motion, links, resume download, SEO, and console errors are checked.
11. No privileged credentials are exposed in the client bundle or Git.
12. Report only tests actually run and deployment status actually achieved.

IMPLEMENTATION METHOD — SIX PHASES
Work through these phases in order. Complete one phase, run checks, fix issues, summarize changed files, setup instructions, actual test results, and remaining risks, then STOP and wait for my approval before beginning the next phase. Do not skip a phase or pretend a check passed.

PHASE 1 — Repository and UI foundation
Inspect repository; establish React/Vite/TypeScript, Tailwind, routing, design tokens, shared components, responsive public/admin route skeletons, and .env.example. Use placeholder-only content and keep it unpublished. Run the app and production build. Stop for approval.

PHASE 2 — Supabase and security
Create SQL migrations, admin allowlist, safe authorization helper, RLS, private/public storage policies, trusted publish function, and security tests. Explain how I create the Supabase project and first admin without sharing secrets in chat. Run available tests. Stop for approval.

PHASE 3 — Admin CMS
